[{:seon.task/name "inspect-a-kept-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Runs are kept (target/check/<time>/runs, runs/, t5/out/*.store) and are evidence. A root opens a kept store for inspection: its reconciler observes and acts on nothing (no spawn, no kill, no judge) unless released, so a run's main and branches can be queried and its agents restored without resuming its work: `bin/root --inspect STORE`, with the MCP tools working against it. Nothing exists yet (bin/root has no --inspect; the proof is unwritten)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/an-inspected-run-starts-nothing}}
 {:seon.task/name "restore-and-replay-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Datahike keeps every commit as an immutable value with its parents (`datahike.versioning/branch-history`; `branch!` accepts a commit id; `commit-as-db` loads one): on a copy of t5/out/real3.store, `d/branch!` from the middle of task-t5-edit-forms's 19 commits (335 evals) gave a branch holding exactly its 29 evals. So branching from any commit costs no copy, and an eval between commits is reached by branching from the commit before and replaying. Restore: an agent forked on a kept branch at its end state (resume's load of the branch program). Replay to step N: an agent forked from the run's basis evaluates its recorded forms 1..N in order through its own REPL, every effect answered from its recorded outcome by a replay owner (nothing fires); each result is compared with its record and a divergence recorded (nondeterminism, or a gap in the record). The debugging agent works the re-creation through seon_eval, its new instructions a task whose :evidence names the source run, branch and point. Needs every form's exact text (`seon.repl/eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole`, widened to every form) and effect outcomes recorded whole (the model's reply is on the turn; file and other effects' answers are not). Nothing built yet."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.server/inspect-a-kept-run" "seon.repl/eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-replayed-agent-reproduces-its-recorded-results
                      seon.server-test/a-divergence-on-replay-is-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "reading-the-context-is-recorded"
  :seon.task/plan "An outside agent working a task through the server (attach, context, eval, done) is recorded by the system like a Seon agent: the run on main, each eval on its branch with origin :outside, the judgment at done. The `:context` op in `seon.server/ops` now submits the block's print to the agent with origin :context, so the read is recorded on the agent's branch (renders refer to it rather than repeat it: ``seon.render-test/a-context-read-is-shown-once``). What remains: the read as a turn of the same shape as a model turn (the exact text returned), so an outside session reads, replays and mines like a Seon run, and this task's proof. A few lines in the server; no harness."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-context-read-is-a-recorded-turn}} {:seon.task/name "the-context-op-answers-the-text"
  :seon.task/plan "Done in code: the `:context` op in `seon.server/ops` submits `(print (seon.turn/context-block task run))` and answers the agent's `:out`, the block's text once, so an outside agent reads exactly what the model reads; `seon.server/mcp-reply` answers seon_context with that text. Before, it answered `(:printed ...)` of the string value, so the block came back pr-str-quoted, then printed again in the answer map (every quote escaped two levels deep). Open: the proof `seon.server-test/the-context-op-answers-the-models-text` is not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/the-context-op-answers-the-models-text}}
 {:seon.task/name "attach-writes-nothing-when-it-is-refused"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: `seon.server/attach!` spawns the agent first (`seon.root/spawn!`) and only once it exists writes the env (model outside), the release and the run in one transaction, so a refused spawn writes nothing and the reconciler (`seon.reconcile/reconciler-proc`) never sees a release with no run and starts a second agent for it. Before, attach wrote env and :execution :running before spawning, so an error left the task's desired state changed, and the reconciler could fork its own agent onto the branch first (attach's spawn then refused: \"branch ... is held by live agent :a1\")."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-refused-attach-writes-nothing}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-server-answers-mcp-itself"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: root's server speaks MCP itself on its one port, telling framing per line: a line opening `{\"` is JSON-RPC (`seon.server/mcp-reply`: initialize, tools/list from the one table `seon.server/ops`, tools/call through `seon.server/answer`), every other line is line EDN. bin/mcp is a relay in stock perl, one connection per message, so a root restart (bin/root next) costs a Claude session nothing; a message root does not answer (not listening, died mid-request) gets the JSON-RPC error naming why, never a hang. Why: the old bin/mcp ran `./seon mcp`, booting the whole binary per Claude session only to relay lines: 729 MB physical footprint each, 7 sessions about 5 GB, past `:seon.config.machine/memory-mb`. v1 had the same cost (one JVM per client). The field's way: an MCP stdio server is newline-delimited JSON-RPC, so any byte pipe to a server that speaks it will do. Deleted `seon mcp`'s mode, src/seon/mcp.clj and its failure-points entry (the connect edge moved to bin/mcp, outside src)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/the-server-speaks-mcp}}
 {:seon.task/name "health-through-the-server"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.server/ops` has :agents :eval :context :attach :done. :agents answers live agents plus every task on main (230+, mostly cancelled), too much for an outside agent's context, and nothing answers health through MCP (v1's runtime_status), which only the dashboard shows. Correct: a :status op (MCP tool seon_status) that answers root's AI render, `seon.render.ai/system`, the text /context.txt serves; :agents lists live agents and running, held or blocked tasks, the rest as `seon.print/bounded`'s count and the form that lists them. One entry each in the ops table; nothing new beside it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/status-is-roots-ai-render seon.server-test/agents-lists-live-work}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-mcp-check-records-both-evals-outside"
  :seon.task/plan "Done: the agent's records were there; root's were not found. Since 15d5c2c7f (an agent is one entity per boot, root is its own entity) seon.server/root-eval records root's evaluations on {:seon.root/id :root} (seon.repl/record-tx), while the check's reader (seon.check/eval-records) still looked root up as [:seon.agent/id :root], an entity no producer writes. The producer is right; the reader now takes the evaluator's identity, [:seon.agent/id a] or [:seon.root/id :root]. Original report: Reproduction: Found 2026-10-23 by the cutover's decider: red in a built scratch worktree of main 4bc598dc1 as well as in the fresh clone, so it is main's, not the cutover's. `bin/check mcp-proof` (`seon.check/mcp-proof`, test/seon/check.clj:1186) prints MCP FAILED: `:both-recorded-outside` is false (the agent eval and the root eval must both be recorded `:outside`); the root-down probe reports five `root's server on 127.0.0.1:NNNNN is not listening (Connection refused)`, which that check expects, and every other check (agent-eval, root-eval, tools-are-the-ops, relay-within-bound, notification-unanswered) is true. Find which of the two evals is not recorded `:outside` (read the recorded evaluations' origin); the report asked for a fix at the producer, but the producer was right and the reader stale. Replaces nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "done-reads-its-reason-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the guard audit of 2026-10-25 (docs/failure-points.md, S14; still true in src/seon/server.clj:171-179). What exists: the :done op waits with a pred that tests the run status and c/defect, and after the wait tests the same two facts again to choose its answer (status in #{:running :ended} and defect, at 174 and 178). Correct: the wait returns the reason it ended on (the pred returns the verdict or the defect it matched), and :done answers that, so one read of the facts decides. Replaces the second read. Proof to write: :done answers the defect the wait matched even when main holds a later commit."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-tools-are-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: the MCP tools are thin wrappers around Seon's REPL-based eval, so an outside session (a Claude) works as a Seon agent does and sees what it sees. Inside, a Seon agent has one tool, eval (prose and code at its REPL), and every capability is a function it evaluates: (my.agent/prove), (my.agent/messages), (my.agent/problem sig), (my.agent/help ..), @ctx; each answer is one shape by the one printer (handle, value or error, :out, and a line of what changed: :main). Rule: every capability is a function an eval calls, none is only a tool. Outside: seon_eval {agent, prose, code} (an agent's id or root) is the tool, through seon.repl/eval-string, origin :outside, its text the printed response a Seon agent's model reads; seon_attach {task} stays (a session needs a process, and attach records its client as holder); the ops agents, context, message, prove and done become functions evaluated on root or the agent ((my.root/agents), (my.agent/context), (my.agent/send-message ..), (my.agent/prove), (my.task/done!)) and their seon.server ops and op-answer methods are deleted, bin/ scripts and checks calling seon_eval. Each answer adds what its caller cannot see otherwise: for a Seon agent, the problems its eval caused (a test child that died in its prove, a refused write, a failed effect: root's records against that eval); for an outside client (bin/mcp stamps its client id), root's problem facts changed on main since that client's last answer (the main t it last saw, held by the server as a cache keyed by client, regenerable: read through Datahike's history, never my.root/problems' ~600 MB recomputation), as problems-model's grouped entries. A worktree's root is one more bin/mcp entry (its port argument), no new mechanism. Also from the 2026-10-30 proposal: a dead agent's end, exit and stderr tail answered by the agents function (root records them, seon.root's end-agent and record-death); one named test proved through the prove function (names take ns/test as bin/test does). Owned by the optimization session with the context session (the owner, 2026-10-30)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "outside-input-has-a-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by AGENTS section 3 step 4 its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: input from outside is parsed and dispatched with no Malli contract on its shape. seon.server/answer takes :map; respond sniffs the protocol from the line's first characters; each op's arguments are declared as JSON Schema in ops, a second schema language never used on an EDN request. seon.web's /call reads args by edn/read-string with no guard, checked first inside the web proc's step, so a malformed request is a step's throw, not a 400. seon.model's sse-reply, reply-of, wire-message(s) and request-body answer or take :map. my.root/example-txs re-states the session shape as a hand predicate (b5fabd00f0 added an arm). The change: :seon.server/request [:multi {:dispatch :op}] validated in respond, a request that is invalid answered, explained, with seon.contract/problems, and the MCP tool list's inputSchema derived from it (malli.json-schema), deleting the hand JSON Schema; the web edge reads args through the same validator and answers 400 with the problems; :seon.model/wire-reply (only the keys read) and :seon.model/wire-request; one :seon.context/session schema (the first transaction's agent requires :seon.agent/id and an example/ key) as seon.context/session-tx's output contract, with example-txs refusing by it, deleting the predicate. Docs: docs/model.md, docs/dashboard.md, the server's section."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-malformed-request-is-answered-with-its-problems}}]
